---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import { frontendSeries } from '../../data/series';
const inlineStyle = `
/* ── Hero ── */
.fe19-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#6c5ce7 0%,#a29bfe 50%,#fd79a8 100%)}

/* ── Env file stack animation ── */
.env-art{margin:2.5rem auto 0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:0.5rem}
.env-file{font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.75rem;padding:0.4rem 1.2rem;border-radius:0.5rem;border:0.0625rem solid rgba(255,255,255,0.3);background:rgba(255,255,255,0.1);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);animation:envStack 0.4s ease both}
.env-file:nth-child(1){animation-delay:0.1s}
.env-file:nth-child(2){animation-delay:0.25s;background:rgba(255,255,255,0.18)}
.env-file:nth-child(3){animation-delay:0.4s}
.env-file:nth-child(4){animation-delay:0.55s}
.env-winner{font-size:0.7rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.25rem 0.75rem;border-radius:1rem;background:#55efc4;color:#1a1a2e;animation:envStack 0.4s ease 0.7s both}
@keyframes envStack{from{opacity:0;transform:translateX(-12px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}

.fe19-num{color:#6c5ce7;background:#f0efff}

/* ── Priority table ── */
.env-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:1.25rem;font-size:0.88rem}
.env-table th{background:#f0efff;color:#6c5ce7;padding:0.6rem 0.9rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:0.125rem solid #ddd}
.env-table td{padding:0.55rem 0.9rem;border-bottom:0.0625rem solid #eee;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.82rem}
.env-table tr:nth-child(even) td{background:#fafafa}
.env-table .wins{color:#00b894;font-weight:700}
.env-table .purpose{font-family:inherit;font-size:0.82rem}

/* ── VITE_ prefix box ── */
.prefix-box{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:1.25rem}
.prefix-tag{padding:0.5rem 1.1rem;border-radius:0.625rem;font-family:'Fira Code',monospace;font-size:0.82rem;font-weight:600}
.prefix-yes{background:#e8f8f5;color:#00b894;border:0.0625rem solid #b2dfdb}
.prefix-no{background:#fdf2ef;color:#e17055;border:0.0625rem solid #ffcc80}
.prefix-arrow{color:#aaa;font-size:1rem}

/* ── Quiz theming ep19 ── */
.quiz-ep19f .quiz-q-num{color:#6c5ce7;background:#f0efff}
.quiz-ep19f .quiz-reveal-btn{color:#6c5ce7}
.quiz-ep19f .quiz-opt-letter{color:#6c5ce7}
.quiz-ep19f .quiz-option:hover{border-color:#6c5ce7;background:#f0efff}
.quiz-ep19f .quiz-answer-highlight{background:#f0efff;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7}
`;
const bodyHtml = `

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero fe19-hero">
  <div class="hero-badge">Episode 19 of 22</div>
  <h1>Environment Variables<br>&amp; .env Files</h1>
  <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.95)">The same app code, three different environments: UAT, staging, production. Different API URLs, different feature flags, different secrets. .env files are how the app knows where it is.</p>
  <div class="env-art">
    <div class="env-file">.env</div>
    <div class="env-file">.env.local &nbsp;&nbsp;← highest priority</div>
    <div class="env-file">.env.latest</div>
    <div class="env-file">.env.production</div>
    <div class="env-winner">npm run dev → loads .env.local first</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe19-num">01</span>
  <h2>What Is an Environment Variable?</h2>
  <p>An <span class="highlight">environment variable</span> is a named value that lives outside your code. Instead of hardcoding an API URL directly in a component, you put it in a .env file and reference it by name.</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// ✗ Hardcoded — wrong approach</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> url = <span class="code-string">'https://api.acvauctions.com/deals'</span>

<span class="code-comment">// ✓ Environment variable — correct approach</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> url = import.meta.env.VITE_DEAL_API_URI + <span class="code-string">'/deals'</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Why is this better? The same code file now works in any environment. The variable's <em>value</em> is different per environment; the <em>code</em> is identical. When the app builds for UAT, <code style="font-size:0.875rem">VITE_DEAL_API_URI</code> points to the UAT server. When it builds for production, it points to the production server.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe19-num">02</span>
  <h2>The .env Files in dealmaker-ui</h2>
  <p>The repo has multiple .env files, each targeting a different environment:</p>

  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment"># .env — default values for every environment</span>
VITE_BASE_URI=latest.acvauctions.com
VITE_PORT=4173
APP_KEY=LOCALDLMKR

<span class="code-comment"># .env.local — your local overrides (highest priority, git-ignored)</span>
VITE_BASE_URI=localhost:8080
VITE_PORT=8080
APP_KEY=LOCALDLMKR
VITE_DEAL_FINDER_API_URI=...  <span class="code-comment">← extra variable only in local</span>

<span class="code-comment"># .env.latest — UAT environment</span>
VITE_BASE_URI=latest.acvauctions.com
APP_KEY=UATDLMKR

<span class="code-comment"># .env.staging — staging environment</span>
VITE_BASE_URI=staging.acvauctions.com

<span class="code-comment"># .env.production — production environment</span>
VITE_BASE_URI=acvauctions.com</pre>
  </div>

  <p>Each script in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">package.json</code> tells Vite which mode to use, which determines which .env file loads:</p>
  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-string">"scripts"</span>: {
  <span class="code-string">"dev"</span>:         <span class="code-string">"vite"</span>,                          <span class="code-comment">// loads .env + .env.local</span>
  <span class="code-string">"build:latest"</span>: <span class="code-string">"vite build --mode latest"</span>,  <span class="code-comment">// loads .env + .env.latest</span>
  <span class="code-string">"build:staging"</span>:<span class="code-string">"vite build --mode staging"</span>, <span class="code-comment">// loads .env + .env.staging</span>
  <span class="code-string">"build:prod"</span>:  <span class="code-string">"vite build --mode production"</span> <span class="code-comment">// loads .env + .env.production</span>
}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe19-num">03</span>
  <h2>Priority Order — Which File Wins?</h2>
  <p>When multiple .env files exist, Vite merges them. If the same variable appears in more than one file, the <span class="highlight">higher priority file wins</span>.</p>

  <table class="env-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>File</th><th>Priority</th><th class="purpose">Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>.env.local</td><td class="wins">highest</td><td class="purpose">Your personal overrides — never committed to git</td></tr>
      <tr><td>.env.[mode]</td><td>high</td><td class="purpose">Mode-specific values (.env.latest, .env.staging, etc.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>.env.[mode].local</td><td>high</td><td class="purpose">Mode-specific personal overrides (rare)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>.env</td><td>lowest</td><td class="purpose">Default fallback values for all environments</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">When you run <code style="font-size:0.875rem">npm run dev</code>, Vite loads <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env</code> first, then <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env.local</code> on top. Any variable in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env.local</code> overrides the same variable in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env</code>.</p>
  <p>This is why running the repo locally points to <code style="font-size:0.875rem">localhost:8080</code> — <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env.local</code> overrides the default <code style="font-size:0.875rem">latest.acvauctions.com</code> from <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env</code>.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe19-num">04</span>
  <h2>The VITE_ Prefix — What Gets Exposed to the Browser?</h2>
  <p>There is an important security rule: <span class="highlight">only variables prefixed with <code style="font-size:0.875rem">VITE_</code> are included in the browser bundle</span>. Variables without the prefix are only available at build time (in Node.js) — they never reach the browser.</p>

  <div class="prefix-box">
    <div class="prefix-tag prefix-yes">VITE_BASE_URI=localhost:8080</div>
    <div class="prefix-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="prefix-tag prefix-yes">available in browser via import.meta.env</div>
  </div>
  <div class="prefix-box" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
    <div class="prefix-tag prefix-no">APP_KEY=LOCALDLMKR</div>
    <div class="prefix-arrow">&#10132;</div>
    <div class="prefix-tag prefix-no">NOT in browser — build-time only</div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">In a Vue component, you read VITE_ variables using <code style="font-size:0.875rem">import.meta.env</code>:</p>
  <div class="code-block">
    <pre><span class="code-comment">// In any Vue component or JS file</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> apiBase = import.meta.env.VITE_BASE_URI
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> apiUrl  = <span class="code-string">\`https://\${apiBase}/api/deals\`</span>

<span class="code-comment">// import.meta.env also gives you built-in values:</span>
import.meta.env.MODE      <span class="code-comment">// 'development', 'latest', 'production', etc.</span>
import.meta.env.PROD      <span class="code-comment">// true if mode is 'production'</span>
import.meta.env.DEV       <span class="code-comment">// true if mode is 'development'</span></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num fe19-num">05</span>
  <h2>Why .env Files Are Git-Ignored</h2>
  <p><code style="font-size:0.875rem">.env.local</code> is always in <code style="font-size:0.875rem">.gitignore</code>. This is intentional and important.</p>

  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1rem;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">What gets committed</strong><br>
      <span style="font-size:0.9rem">.env, .env.latest, .env.staging, .env.production — these contain non-secret config like API hostnames. Safe to share with the team.</span>
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.1rem 1.25rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">What stays local</strong><br>
      <span style="font-size:0.9rem">.env.local — your personal overrides. If it ever contains a real secret (API key, token), it must never enter git history.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-top:1.25rem">A critical point: even though <code style="font-size:0.875rem">VITE_</code> variables end up in the browser bundle, they are not truly secret. Anyone can open DevTools and read them. <span class="highlight">Never put real secrets (passwords, private API keys) in VITE_ variables.</span> Those belong server-side only.</p>
</section>

<!-- RECAP -->
<section class="section reveal">
  <span class="section-num">RECAP</span>
  <h2>What We Learned</h2>
  <div style="display:grid;gap:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">
    <div style="background:#f0efff;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #6c5ce7">
      <strong style="color:#6c5ce7">Environment variables</strong> — named values outside the code. Same code, different values per environment.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e3f2fd;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #0984e3">
      <strong style="color:#0984e3">Priority order</strong> — .env.local wins over .env.[mode] wins over .env. Running dev locally loads .env.local, which overrides defaults.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#e8f8f5;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #00b894">
      <strong style="color:#00b894">VITE_ prefix</strong> — only variables prefixed VITE_ reach the browser. Others stay build-time only. Read in code via import.meta.env.
    </div>
    <div style="background:#fdf2ef;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;border-radius:0.875rem;border-left:0.25rem solid #e17055">
      <strong style="color:#e17055">.env.local is git-ignored</strong> — personal overrides stay off git. VITE_ variables in the browser are public — never put real secrets there.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKPOINT QUIZ -->
<section class="section reveal quiz-ep19f">
  <span class="section-num">CHECKPOINT</span>
  <h2>Probe Your Thinking</h2>
  <p class="quiz-subtitle">No pressure. Just see what stuck.</p>

  <div class="quiz-grid">

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 01</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">When you run <code>npm run dev</code>, the app points to localhost instead of UAT. Why?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Because dev mode always uses localhost by default</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Because .env.local overrides .env — it sets VITE_BASE_URI to localhost:8080, which takes priority</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Because Vite ignores .env files during development</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Because npm run dev runs a different version of the code than npm run build</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) Because .env.local overrides .env — it sets VITE_BASE_URI to localhost:8080, which takes priority</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">.env defines VITE_BASE_URI=latest.acvauctions.com (UAT). .env.local defines VITE_BASE_URI=localhost:8080. When you run npm run dev, both files load but .env.local has higher priority, so the local value wins. This is the intended behavior — each developer's machine points to their own local backend during development.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 02</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">What is the VITE_ prefix for, and can users see VITE_ variables?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>VITE_ marks variables as secret — they are hidden from users</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>VITE_ marks variables to include in the browser bundle — yes, users can see them in DevTools</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>VITE_ marks variables that are only used by Vite itself during the build</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>VITE_ marks variables that are sent to the server on every API call</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(B) VITE_ marks variables to include in the browser bundle — yes, users can see them in DevTools</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">Variables without VITE_ stay build-time only — they never reach the browser. Variables with VITE_ get inlined into the JavaScript bundle. Since the bundle is downloaded by the browser, anyone can open DevTools, search the source, and read these values. They are not secrets. Never put API keys, passwords, or private tokens in VITE_ variables.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-card">
    <div class="quiz-card-header"><span class="quiz-q-num">Q 03</span></div>
    <p class="quiz-question">Why is .env.local in .gitignore?</p>
    <ul class="quiz-options">
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">A</span>Because .env.local is generated automatically and doesn't need to be tracked</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">B</span>Because it contains production secrets that must never be committed</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" data-correct="true" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">C</span>Because it contains personal overrides specific to one developer's machine — other developers should not receive them</button></li>
      <li><button class="quiz-option" type="button"><span class="quiz-opt-letter">D</span>Because Vite ignores it if it's tracked in git</button></li>
    </ul>
    <button class="quiz-reveal-btn" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Show Answer <span class="quiz-chevron">▾</span></button>
    <div class="quiz-answer">
      <div class="quiz-answer-inner">
        <div class="quiz-answer-highlight">✓ Correct: <strong>(C) Because it contains personal overrides specific to one developer's machine — other developers should not receive them</strong></div>
        <p class="quiz-answer-text">.env.local is for your machine only. If you point VITE_BASE_URI at a local backend running on port 8080, that path only works on your computer. Committing it would break other developers' setups when they pull. And if the file ever contains real credentials, keeping it off git prevents accidental exposure in version history.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- NEXT -->
<section class="next-part" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#6c5ce7,#fd79a8)">
  <h2>Ready for Episode 20?</h2>
  <p>The app has a login page. But what stops someone from typing /dashboard directly in the URL bar? Route guards — the frontend's first line of defense.</p>
  <a href="part20.html" class="btn btn-white">Continue to Episode 20 &#10132;</a>
</section>
`;
---
<ArticleLayout title="How the Frontend Works — Part 19: Environment Variables | Unbox the Black Box" inlineStyle={inlineStyle} series={frontendSeries} episodeNum={19} hasQuiz={true}>
<Fragment set:html={bodyHtml} />
</ArticleLayout>
